WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.135 --> 00:00:07.405
The internet is an absolute miracle.

00:00:07.872 --> 00:00:09.940
And for the last 30 years,

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:13.177
it has been driven by [a] very consistent

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.146
and fundamental business model

00:00:15.146 --> 00:00:18.516
that has given us everything
amazing about the internet

00:00:18.549 --> 00:00:21.218
and everything not so amazing as well.

00:00:21.252 --> 00:00:25.122
I run a company that provides a lot
of the internet's infrastructure

00:00:25.156 --> 00:00:27.458
and as a result, means three things.

00:00:27.491 --> 00:00:33.831
One, we've had this unique view
into what is going on online.

00:00:34.598 --> 00:00:39.336
Two, we care enormously
about the success of the internet.

00:00:39.703 --> 00:00:42.173
And three, I can share with you

00:00:42.206 --> 00:00:45.342
some of how that underlying
business model is changing

00:00:45.376 --> 00:00:47.044
and why it's important.

00:00:47.078 --> 00:00:49.447
Because as that business model changes,

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.117
it's going to have a dramatic impact
on what the internet is,

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.654
but beyond that,
on what content is created,

00:00:56.654 --> 00:00:58.589
on what journalists get compensated for,

00:00:58.622 --> 00:01:01.258
on how researchers are able
to do their research.

00:01:01.292 --> 00:01:02.760
And so it's up to all of us

00:01:02.793 --> 00:01:05.729
as that business model
of the internet changes,

00:01:05.763 --> 00:01:08.999
to make sure it changes
for something we want

00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:12.069
and can learn from the mistakes
that we had in the past.

00:01:12.069 --> 00:01:15.372
So to review the past, let's look
at what that business model was.

00:01:15.406 --> 00:01:18.976
And we all know it basically,
which was: the internet was built on

00:01:19.009 --> 00:01:21.745
we took an idea, and we put it online,

00:01:21.779 --> 00:01:25.349
and then we drove traffic to that idea.

00:01:25.716 --> 00:01:28.752
And then from that traffic,
we either sold things,

00:01:28.786 --> 00:01:31.055
subscriptions or ads.

00:01:31.522 --> 00:01:34.058
And it's been pretty amazing to watch

00:01:34.058 --> 00:01:38.062
how that has very much driven
the growth of the web

00:01:38.095 --> 00:01:40.064
and the growth of the internet over time.

00:01:40.064 --> 00:01:43.000
And it survived a number
of dramatic platform shifts.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.336
We all started browsing
the internet on browsers,

00:01:45.336 --> 00:01:46.871
on our desktop or laptop.

00:01:46.904 --> 00:01:49.807
That transitioned
to be increasingly social media,

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.977
which was the interface
through which we consumed content.

00:01:53.444 --> 00:01:56.280
And then we went to consuming it
through our mobile devices.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.315
And yet, through all of this,

00:01:58.315 --> 00:02:01.785
the humble banner ad
has basically financed it all.

00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:04.788
Traffic has been the currency
of the internet,

00:02:04.788 --> 00:02:09.426
and that's what everyone has built
their content to try to achieve.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.096
And if we want to look
for who's the Superman,

00:02:12.096 --> 00:02:15.232
who's the superhero that has driven this

00:02:15.266 --> 00:02:17.535
for the last 27 years,

00:02:17.535 --> 00:02:19.670
that has been Google.

00:02:19.703 --> 00:02:23.741
Google built not only the search engine
to index how the internet works,

00:02:23.741 --> 00:02:26.710
but then they built
the monetization system

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:31.048
to actually ensure
that content creators got paid

00:02:31.081 --> 00:02:33.484
for the traffic that they generated.

00:02:33.517 --> 00:02:37.221
The internet owes a ton
to Google in the last 27 years.

00:02:37.721 --> 00:02:39.023
But the world is changing.

00:02:39.056 --> 00:02:41.592
AI is the topic du jour,

00:02:41.625 --> 00:02:43.928
and what I think a lot of people
don't understand

00:02:43.961 --> 00:02:46.430
is that AI is yet another platform shift.

00:02:46.463 --> 00:02:50.267
Think about how much more of the way
that you consume information already

00:02:50.301 --> 00:02:52.069
is looking at your AI chatbot,

00:02:52.102 --> 00:02:55.406
and how much more of that
will be the case in the future.

00:02:55.439 --> 00:02:58.509
We can see it in a lot of things
we do kind of normally,

00:02:58.542 --> 00:03:02.680
like, this is a Google search
from the early days of Google.

00:03:02.713 --> 00:03:03.881
Not even that long ago,

00:03:03.914 --> 00:03:07.351
where I would type something in,
like, when was the first TED conference?

00:03:07.384 --> 00:03:10.321
And then I would get back
basically a treasure map

00:03:10.354 --> 00:03:12.456
where I would have
to continue to do the work

00:03:12.489 --> 00:03:14.525
and click on the links, generating traffic

00:03:14.525 --> 00:03:16.193
to find what my answer was.

00:03:16.227 --> 00:03:19.496
The search wasn't over
when the results returned.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.733
That was just the beginning,
and you had to follow the treasure map.

00:03:22.766 --> 00:03:24.435
That's what a search engine is.

00:03:24.468 --> 00:03:26.337
Today we have something very different.

00:03:26.337 --> 00:03:29.640
We have something that's closer
to what I would call an answer engine,

00:03:29.673 --> 00:03:32.176
where you're getting the answer
directly on the page.

00:03:32.176 --> 00:03:34.645
And what's amazing about that
is that as a consumer,

00:03:34.678 --> 00:03:36.180
it's better for you.

00:03:36.213 --> 00:03:38.315
But what's terrifying about it

00:03:38.349 --> 00:03:42.152
is that it means traffic isn't going
to the publishers' sites,

00:03:42.186 --> 00:03:44.755
and traffic is the currency
of the internet.

00:03:45.055 --> 00:03:49.126
And so 18 months ago, publishers called us
and said, “Are you seeing this, too?”

00:03:49.126 --> 00:03:50.894
And I have to confess,

00:03:50.894 --> 00:03:52.963
we weren't really paying
a ton of attention,

00:03:52.997 --> 00:03:55.299
but we went into our logs,
we went into our data,

00:03:55.332 --> 00:03:57.268
we pulled out that information and said,

00:03:57.268 --> 00:03:59.803
“How much harder is it to get traffic

00:03:59.837 --> 00:04:02.339
based on the same amount of content today

00:04:02.373 --> 00:04:04.475
than it was with the Google of old?”

00:04:04.508 --> 00:04:07.344
And the answer is, it's 5.2 times harder

00:04:07.344 --> 00:04:10.414
to get traffic for the same amount
of content that was out there.

00:04:10.447 --> 00:04:11.649
But that's the good news.

00:04:11.682 --> 00:04:12.850
That's with Google.

00:04:12.883 --> 00:04:14.818
What is it with OpenAI?

00:04:14.852 --> 00:04:16.520
750 times harder.

00:04:16.553 --> 00:04:17.888
What is it with Anthropic?

00:04:17.888 --> 00:04:20.257
32,000 times harder.

00:04:20.658 --> 00:04:22.626
The answer engines are giving the answers

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.728
and people aren't following the footnotes.

00:04:24.762 --> 00:04:26.297
And this was 18 months ago.

00:04:26.297 --> 00:04:28.799
What’s happened since,
has it gotten better or worse?

00:04:28.799 --> 00:04:32.536
Do you trust AI more today
than you did 18 months ago?

00:04:32.536 --> 00:04:35.973
The answer is most people do,
and as a result,

00:04:36.006 --> 00:04:39.977
it's actually gotten twice as hard
to get traffic to content that's there.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:42.012
You're not following
the footnotes anymore.

00:04:42.046 --> 00:04:45.482
You're actually trusting that AI systems
are giving you the right answer,

00:04:45.482 --> 00:04:46.850
and that's destroying media.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:49.086
Across the board,
we're seeing reported numbers

00:04:49.086 --> 00:04:52.723
... of between a 90 and
30 percent decline

00:04:52.723 --> 00:04:54.358
in the traffic to their sites,

00:04:54.391 --> 00:04:56.960
and this is extremely problematic

00:04:56.994 --> 00:04:59.963
because I think journalists deserve
to be able to eat.

00:04:59.997 --> 00:05:01.799
(Applause)

00:05:01.799 --> 00:05:05.469
And fundamentally,
if the internet is built on traffic

00:05:05.502 --> 00:05:09.540
and this new business model
isn't delivering traffic,

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.109
then I'm not sure how media
is going to survive

00:05:12.142 --> 00:05:14.845
unless we create a new business model.

00:05:14.878 --> 00:05:18.282
And the good news is
the AI companies want journalism.

00:05:18.315 --> 00:05:20.317
They want people out there doing research.

00:05:20.317 --> 00:05:21.719
We all want journalism.

00:05:21.719 --> 00:05:23.654
We want people out there doing research.

00:05:23.687 --> 00:05:26.890
And so I think, I'm actually
kind of optimistic

00:05:26.924 --> 00:05:29.126
that there might be
a solution to this problem.

00:05:29.126 --> 00:05:30.861
And here's the way I think about it.

00:05:30.894 --> 00:05:32.629
For the first time in human history,

00:05:32.663 --> 00:05:35.532
we have a mathematical model
of all of human knowledge.

00:05:35.566 --> 00:05:39.203
It's not perfect, but that's essentially
what all of the LLMs are.

00:05:39.203 --> 00:05:41.705
And I think of it like a giant
block of Swiss cheese.

00:05:41.739 --> 00:05:43.841
There's a lot of cheese,
that's what we know.

00:05:43.874 --> 00:05:46.043
But we also have a lot
of holes in the cheese.

00:05:46.043 --> 00:05:47.745
Those are the things we don't know.

00:05:47.778 --> 00:05:49.346
And the thing that is incredible

00:05:49.346 --> 00:05:51.982
is that what all of you want, what I want,

00:05:51.982 --> 00:05:53.684
what the AI companies want

00:05:53.684 --> 00:05:57.654
is they want people out there
filling in the holes in the cheese.

00:05:58.088 --> 00:06:01.058
They don’t want you writing
stories that already exist --

00:06:01.058 --> 00:06:03.227
they want you actually
creating new knowledge.

00:06:03.227 --> 00:06:05.829
And the AI companies,
I've talked to every one of them,

00:06:05.829 --> 00:06:09.566
they're all willing to actually pay
for people who are creating new knowledge,

00:06:09.566 --> 00:06:12.436
creating new ideas, ideas worth spreading.

00:06:12.436 --> 00:06:14.738
That's what this new system would reward.

00:06:14.772 --> 00:06:20.177
Compare that to what the business model
of media is for the last 10 years,

00:06:20.177 --> 00:06:22.946
which is not filling in
the holes in the cheese,

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:27.818
but instead, basically,
lighting the cheese on fire, right?

00:06:27.851 --> 00:06:29.853
Incendiary cheese, right?

00:06:29.887 --> 00:06:32.990
Yet another story about what happened
at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue,

00:06:33.023 --> 00:06:35.025
told in the most
inflammatory way possible.

00:06:35.058 --> 00:06:36.860
I've sat with media executives

00:06:36.894 --> 00:06:41.565
who brag about the fact that they create
content for as little as possible,

00:06:41.598 --> 00:06:45.502
and then write headlines
that create the largest cortisol response

00:06:45.536 --> 00:06:47.438
so that you will click on them.

00:06:47.471 --> 00:06:49.573
That's not a world
we should be celebrating.

00:06:49.606 --> 00:06:51.942
That's not a world that's making
the world better.

00:06:51.975 --> 00:06:54.077
If, on the other hand,
we actually celebrated

00:06:54.111 --> 00:06:55.946
who is making the cheese bigger,

00:06:55.979 --> 00:06:57.147
who's filling in that --

00:06:57.147 --> 00:07:00.451
Imagine in the future
that you actually had AI companies

00:07:00.451 --> 00:07:01.618
that were measuring

00:07:01.652 --> 00:07:04.822
who actually advanced human
knowledge mathematically

00:07:04.855 --> 00:07:06.857
over the course
of the last year the most,

00:07:06.857 --> 00:07:08.125
and we gave them an award.

00:07:08.125 --> 00:07:09.960
We could call it the Big Cheese award.

00:07:09.993 --> 00:07:10.994
(Laughter)

00:07:11.028 --> 00:07:12.196
I'm not kidding.

00:07:12.229 --> 00:07:13.764
Like, how amazing would that be?

00:07:13.797 --> 00:07:16.567
And you might be thinking,
OK, Matthew, you're crazy.

00:07:16.567 --> 00:07:19.603
Like, I'm the person who thinks
AI is just destroying the world,

00:07:19.636 --> 00:07:21.672
and technology always makes things worse.

00:07:21.672 --> 00:07:24.741
I'm going to share just two anecdotes
that make me have some hope

00:07:24.775 --> 00:07:26.543
that this might actually be the case.

00:07:26.543 --> 00:07:27.978
The first starts 23 years ago.

00:07:28.011 --> 00:07:30.981
23 years ago,
the music industry had a huge problem:

00:07:30.981 --> 00:07:33.617
Napster, Kazaa -- everyone
was stealing music.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:37.254
And then Steve Jobs stepped on stage,
almost 23 years ago to this day,

00:07:37.254 --> 00:07:39.690
and he said, “We’re going to do
something different.

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:41.725
We're going to charge 99 cents per song.

00:07:41.758 --> 00:07:44.261
We're going to actually say
that you should be paying

00:07:44.261 --> 00:07:45.796
for the music that you consume.”

00:07:45.796 --> 00:07:47.865
Now that’s not the business
model that won,

00:07:47.898 --> 00:07:51.268
but it was the start, and it turned
into what is today Spotify

00:07:51.301 --> 00:07:53.403
and all the other systems
that are out there,

00:07:53.437 --> 00:07:56.306
which is basically $10 a month,
all-you-can-eat music,

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:57.508
and it's pretty amazing.

00:07:57.541 --> 00:08:03.647
Last year, Spotify alone sent
over $10 billion to music creators

00:08:03.647 --> 00:08:05.182
in revenue.

00:08:05.682 --> 00:08:09.887
And again, that's in addition
to titles, TikToks, YouTube,

00:08:09.887 --> 00:08:12.256
Apple Music, everybody else,

00:08:12.256 --> 00:08:15.526
more money is going to music creation
than any time in human history.

00:08:15.526 --> 00:08:18.161
So technology can be pie-expanding.

00:08:18.795 --> 00:08:20.631
In fact, I thought it was so important

00:08:20.631 --> 00:08:23.033
that I flew up to Stockholm
to meet with Daniel Ek,

00:08:23.066 --> 00:08:26.303
the founder of Spotify, and he said,
"You know, it's pretty amazing.

00:08:26.303 --> 00:08:30.140
We know when someone goes to Spotify
and they type in Taylor Swift,

00:08:30.173 --> 00:08:31.408
"Shake It Off"

00:08:31.441 --> 00:08:33.710
that we can give them
what they're looking for."

00:08:33.744 --> 00:08:35.779
But every once in a while,
someone comes in

00:08:35.779 --> 00:08:37.147
and types in something like,

00:08:37.180 --> 00:08:40.517
'I want a song about how much
fun it is to dance disco with your cat.'

00:08:40.551 --> 00:08:41.752
(Laughter)

00:08:41.785 --> 00:08:44.021
And nobody's written that.

00:08:44.054 --> 00:08:46.023
But they know that nobody's written that.

00:08:46.056 --> 00:08:48.692
And so they actually go back
and take those queries

00:08:48.725 --> 00:08:50.827
and then publish them back
to music creators.

00:08:50.861 --> 00:08:54.298
And this is the moment you're all
going to think you're doing life wrong.

00:08:54.298 --> 00:08:56.133
But there's a guy who lives in Denmark

00:08:56.166 --> 00:08:57.768
who makes €40 million a year

00:08:57.801 --> 00:09:00.037
writing songs for unfulfilled
Spotify queries.

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:02.072
(Laughter)

00:09:02.105 --> 00:09:04.207
And I think that's beautiful.

00:09:04.241 --> 00:09:06.109
That's filling the holes in the cheese.

00:09:06.109 --> 00:09:07.611
Because when I look for a song,

00:09:07.644 --> 00:09:09.446
what I'm looking for is an emotion.

00:09:09.479 --> 00:09:12.649
And this person is helping me find a song
to resonate that way.

00:09:12.649 --> 00:09:15.352
And I think that's a model
for what could happen as well.

00:09:15.385 --> 00:09:17.821
Another story starts a little
bit closer to home.

00:09:17.854 --> 00:09:21.892
So this is the Park Record newspaper.

00:09:22.993 --> 00:09:25.228
The first place I ever saw
my name in print --

00:09:25.228 --> 00:09:27.464
my hometown newspaper
from Park City, Utah.

00:09:27.464 --> 00:09:28.799
It was dying.

00:09:28.799 --> 00:09:31.568
So three years ago,
my wife and I bought it.

00:09:31.602 --> 00:09:35.038
We did not buy it because we thought
it was going to be a great business.

00:09:35.072 --> 00:09:37.808
We bought it because we thought
local news was important.

00:09:37.808 --> 00:09:39.576
In fact, we had no financial KPIs --

00:09:39.576 --> 00:09:43.480
the only KPI we focused on was how
much could we increase voter turnout.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:46.984
And the answer was, we got it
from the low 20 percent to over 60 percent

00:09:46.984 --> 00:09:48.719
in the last election in three years,

00:09:48.752 --> 00:09:49.920
which is amazing, right?

00:09:49.953 --> 00:09:51.088
(Applause)

00:09:51.121 --> 00:09:52.823
Local news matters.

00:09:52.856 --> 00:09:54.591
But here's the crazy part.

00:09:55.225 --> 00:09:56.994
Terrible business, right?

00:09:57.361 --> 00:09:58.795
Except it's not.

00:09:58.829 --> 00:10:02.032
We're likely going to make more in 2026

00:10:02.065 --> 00:10:04.167
off licensing our content to AI companies

00:10:04.201 --> 00:10:06.637
than we do off digital advertising. Why?

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:08.872
Because if you're traveling
to Park City, Utah,

00:10:08.872 --> 00:10:11.375
and you want to know
what's the great new restaurant,

00:10:11.408 --> 00:10:13.410
what's the cool band
that's coming to town,

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:15.012
we're the only ones that have it.

00:10:15.045 --> 00:10:17.014
So think about how amazing that would be.

00:10:17.014 --> 00:10:18.615
Think about how if in the future,

00:10:18.649 --> 00:10:21.418
the business model
of the new AI-driven internet

00:10:21.418 --> 00:10:24.154
actually rewards the media
we're all craving,

00:10:24.187 --> 00:10:26.823
local, unique, original content.

00:10:27.257 --> 00:10:29.259
That's what I'm super excited about.

00:10:29.893 --> 00:10:31.161
But there are problems.

00:10:31.561 --> 00:10:32.796
Challenges.

00:10:32.829 --> 00:10:35.065
It's kind of like in the superhero films,

00:10:35.098 --> 00:10:39.102
where the hero of the last movie
becomes the villain of the next.

00:10:39.102 --> 00:10:42.239
And so our good friends at Google ...

00:10:42.272 --> 00:10:43.440
Ugh.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:44.741
(Laughter)

00:10:44.775 --> 00:10:46.076
They are holding this back.

00:10:46.109 --> 00:10:49.112
They believe that they have
a God-given right

00:10:49.146 --> 00:10:50.580
to all of the content online.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:52.582
They shouldn't have to pay for any of it.

00:10:52.616 --> 00:10:54.117
And they have a huge advantage.

00:10:54.117 --> 00:10:55.819
In fact, it's hard to believe this,

00:10:55.852 --> 00:10:58.055
but every other AI company
was actually started

00:10:58.055 --> 00:10:59.690
to oppose Google, right?

00:10:59.690 --> 00:11:01.258
That’s what OpenAI started with.

00:11:01.291 --> 00:11:03.393
Anthropic was just
a spinoff of OpenAI, xAI.

00:11:03.427 --> 00:11:07.030
All of this was all to say,
we can't let Google win this entire game.

00:11:07.030 --> 00:11:08.865
How much of an advantage do they have?

00:11:08.899 --> 00:11:10.333
Well we looked at this again,

00:11:10.367 --> 00:11:13.503
and we found that every
single page that OpenAI sees,

00:11:13.537 --> 00:11:15.272
Google sees three.

00:11:15.305 --> 00:11:18.241
Four in Microsoft’s case,

00:11:18.275 --> 00:11:19.743
five in Anthropic's case,

00:11:19.743 --> 00:11:22.846
21 or 22 in xAI's case.

00:11:22.879 --> 00:11:25.382
An enormous advantage that Google has,

00:11:25.382 --> 00:11:27.417
and they want to hold
on to that advantage.

00:11:27.451 --> 00:11:29.453
In fact, they're playing incredibly dirty.

00:11:29.453 --> 00:11:31.154
They're saying to content creators,

00:11:31.188 --> 00:11:34.024
"You can't stop us
from training on your content

00:11:34.057 --> 00:11:36.393
or we will take you out of search."

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:38.562
Horrible.

00:11:38.595 --> 00:11:40.664
Superman, how far you’ve fallen!

00:11:40.697 --> 00:11:42.466
(Laughter)

00:11:42.899 --> 00:11:45.569
And so I was pretty depressed,
I have to confess,

00:11:45.602 --> 00:11:48.371
thinking we were on the cusp
of creating something amazing:

00:11:48.405 --> 00:11:51.141
a better internet.

00:11:51.575 --> 00:11:56.446
And yet, I have to just realize
that no AI company's top priority

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.081
is paying content creators.

00:11:58.648 --> 00:12:00.283
What's their top priority?

00:12:01.318 --> 00:12:02.719
Beating Google.

00:12:03.153 --> 00:12:04.855
Which gave me an idea.

00:12:05.655 --> 00:12:07.557
Kind of a wonderful idea.

00:12:08.125 --> 00:12:10.794
An idea which is going on
right now, and it's working.

00:12:10.827 --> 00:12:12.562
Which is:

00:12:12.596 --> 00:12:14.931
don’t charge for all
the content that’s out there,

00:12:14.965 --> 00:12:18.769
just charge the AI companies for
the ability to catch Google.

00:12:19.269 --> 00:12:21.104
And I don't think that's the end game,

00:12:21.138 --> 00:12:23.373
but I think it might be the iTunes moment.

00:12:23.373 --> 00:12:27.244
It might be the point that shows us
content is worth paying for.

00:12:27.277 --> 00:12:28.678
Everyone should be out there,

00:12:28.678 --> 00:12:31.548
and we can create a better
business model for the internet.

00:12:31.548 --> 00:12:35.085
And so I'm hopeful that on this stage,
not too long from now,

00:12:35.118 --> 00:12:37.621
I get to hand out one of the first
Big Cheese awards,

00:12:37.621 --> 00:12:38.922
and I really appreciate it.

00:12:38.955 --> 00:12:40.157
Thank you.

